WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.120
First thing you’re struck by is the landscape, 

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.520
like it’s absolutely stunning the entire way.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.640
The landscape changes so quickly 

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.320
from one amazing vista to the next. 

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.480
As a photographer, 
like you can’t really ask for anything else.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.040
There’s a lot of inspiring places on the planet,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:28.840
but what I find so special about here
is the variety of landscapes, of things you can see. 

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.520
We’ve seen the beach, 
we’ve been swimming with dolphins in the ocean 

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.160
and then next day we’re in a vineyard in rolling hills.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.560
We were able to have a bunch of different experiences 
in a very short period of time.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.360
My favorite experience in Kaikoura 
was swimming with the dolphins.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.920
There had to be like thirty dolphins 
and it was mesmerizing.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.560
I must have yelped a dozen times, cause every time 
they came up it was like a squeal of delight.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.320
I’ve never been that close with wildlife.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.600
I was incredibly moved by the experience.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.160
Really close to Nelson is this amazing 
national park called Abel Tasman.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.280
All of a sudden you’re overlooking 
this amazing body of water with this huge cliff.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.680
I don’t think that I’ve seen that kind of a magical beach in my life.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.640
Like, jaw dropping.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.800
The water literally had like eight different 
shades of colours.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:39.200
I got to meet one of the local guides Lee-Anne, 
she was amazing.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.400
She wanted you to feel instantly 
what was so special for her in the landscape.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.280
There seems to be something with the 
people and the landscape,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:55.560
that work in sync and I think 
this is something that is so real and intense and beautiful.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.960
New Zealanders are warm…

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.320
They do go away leaving here as part as my family.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.560
Welcoming.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.000
We love sharing this place with people.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.040
Very open.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.000
Awesome.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.240
- Here we go team!
- Wow!

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.480
You feel a friendship and a feeling 
of family right from the get go.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.240
Mauri Ora!

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.800
[glasses clink]

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.160
The welcoming spirit seem to really be
the defining element of New Zealand culture.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.480
They love their surroundings, and they love to share it.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.840
They want to know where you’re from, 
they want to know how you’re enjoying the country.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.240
Like, each person that I encountered 
in that market place, and like it was really genuine.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.440
And this spirit really went through 
all the people we met, all the regions. 

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.960
There seems to be the mix of pride 
and welcome that felt truly special as a traveler.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.400
People have gone out of their way to help me understand, 

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.360
and talk about their history, their culture.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.480
So in Rotorua I visited the national carving school,

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.080
and James, he was like this little fountain of little stories, 

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.440
telling me and giving me a much 
better introduction to that world.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:10.320
I learnt about the craft, but much more importantly 
I learnt about the cultural meaning of the carvings.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.800
So there’s a world that we learned 
in coming here called manakitanga. 

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.800
To me what that means 
it is that its an honor to be honoring you.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.320
You’re always sharing the meals with somebody,
we’re talking about culture, 

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.560
we’re talking about family, 
we’re talking about the things that are important.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.240
Those personal connections. It wasn’t just a welcome,
it made me feel a part of things here.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.800
I would definitely people to go to New Zealand. 

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.360
Come and try to experience the nature
through the eyes and the hearts of the people.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:48.760
I think it’s really easy to go to places where 
culture is put on display as a sort of tourist attraction

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.120
and this was so real.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:57.000
I was really touched by how authentic it was.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.840
New Zealand at some point affects you,
in the most beautiful way.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.240
I want to come back very soon.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.240
I am in love with this place. 

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.280
I’m in love with this place.

